.TH STUDENT-LS 1 "October 2026" "1.6.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
student-ls \- list directory contents for Operating Systems PA-02
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./bin/ls
.RB [ \-lxR ]
.RI [ FILE|DIRECTORY ... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
List non-hidden directory entries in case-sensitive byte order.
With no operand, list the current directory.
The default layout fills columns down, then across.
Column width is the longest filename plus two spaces.
The terminal width is detected with ioctl; the fallback is 80 columns.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-l
Use aligned long format: permissions, link count, owner, group, size,
modification time and filename. Symbolic links include their targets.
.TP
.B \-x
Fill columns across, then down. The last of \-l and \-x wins.
.TP
.B \-R
List child directories recursively after the current listing.
Symbolic links to directories are not followed.
.TP
.B \-\-
End option parsing, allowing operands beginning with a hyphen.
.SH COLORS
Directories are blue, executable regular files green, archives ending in
.tar, .gz or .zip red, symbolic links pink/magenta, and special files reverse video.
Colors are also emitted when output is redirected. Executable bits take
precedence over archive extensions.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 on filesystem or output errors; 2 for invalid options.
.SH LIMITATIONS
This is an educational subset of GNU ls. Hidden-file options, locale collation,
ACL markers, shell quoting and Unicode display-cell measurement are not implemented.
Filename lengths are measured in bytes. Symbolic-link operands are displayed
as links rather than followed. Filesystem races and mount cycles are not handled
as comprehensively as in GNU coreutils.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
make
./bin/ls
./bin/ls -l src
./bin/ls -x .
./bin/ls -lR src
man -l man/ls.1
.fi
